ted">Every commit gets a checkpoint: the code change paired with the full agent session that produced it. Look at any change and the reasoning is right there.</p></div><div class="hidden w-px border-l border-subtle md:block"></div><div><span class="mb-6 block font-mono text-5xl font-bold text-muted/30">02</span><h3 class="mb-2 font-headline text-lg text-default italic">&quot;Where does all this context actually go?&quot;</h3><p class="leading-relaxed text-muted">Nowhere new. Checkpoints are stored directly in your git history. No hosted service, no external database. Just your repo, your workflow, your data.</p></div><div class="hidden w-px border-l border-subtle md:block"></div><div><span class="mb-6 block font-mono text-5xl font-bold text-muted/30">03</span><h3 class="mb-2 font-headline text-lg text-default italic">&quot;Why does the agent keep making the same mistakes?&quot;</h3><p class="leading-relaxed text-muted">Because it starts every session from zero. Checkpoints give agents the full history of how your codebase was built. Not just the code, but the decisions behind it.</p></div></div></div></section><div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-7xl border-t border-subtle"></div><section><div class="mx-auto flex max-w-2xl flex-col items-center text-center"><h2 class="mb-3 font-headline text-3xl text-default sm:text-4xl">Start capturing your intent</h2><p class="mb-8 max-w-xl text-lg text-muted">Entire is open source and free to use. Install it once, and never lose the context behind your commits again.</p><div class="w-full max-w-lg"><div role="group" aria-label="Copy code" class="floating-control-frame w-full rounded-xl h-11"><div class="flex size-full items-stretch rounded-[calc(var(--radius-xl)-1px)] floating-control-surface"><button class="flex h-full cursor-pointer items-center gap-1 rounded-l-lg border-r border-default bg-transparent font-medium transition-colors hover:bg-neutral-100 focus:outline-none focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:-outline-offset-1 focus